From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: HS200 module clock -- the open question, made measurable

HS200 may be running at half the card clock the driver reports, exactly as
SDR104 did until `mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth`. This patch puts HS200 on the doubling branch so that one
board session settles it, and it says plainly that the manual does not settle
it, because it does not.

WHAT THE UM CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
-----------------------------------------
Asked directly of a733-um-v0.92 and answered directly:

- SMHC_NTSR[31] MODE_SEL, 11.3.8.23, offset 0x005C, default 0x8171_0000:
  0 selects the old sample/output timing mode, 1 the new one (default 1).
  That is the entire entry. It is a sample and output *timing* selector.
  No frequency, no ratio.
- 11.3.5.6 (the phase-offset section for command and data) uses MODE_SEL and
  HS400_NEW_SAM_EN as step 1 of choosing *phase offsets*, and Table 11-9 then
  splits the DDR4 row into a 1x mode (0x5c[31]=0) and a 2x mode (0x5c[31]=1)
  and the HS400 row into a 1x mode (0x5c[0]=0) and a 2x mode (0x5c[0]=1). The SDR row
  and the DDR8 row carry no 1x/2x split at all.
- SMHC_CLKDIV[7:0] CCLK_DIV, 11.3.8.2: divides the source
  clock by 2*n (n = 0..255); the field must be 0 whenever HS400_MD_EN is set.
- The one place a factor of two between the card-clock time base and real time
  is documented is the SMHC_TMOUT note, 11.3.8.3: the effective timeout is only half
  of the programmed value in three cases: DDR8 mode; DDR4 with
  SMHC_NTSR[MODE_SELEC](0x5C[31]) high; HS400 with
  SMHC_NTSR[HS400_NEW_SAMPLE_EN](0x5C[0]) high. The timeout is counted in card
  clock periods, so the halving means the counter runs at twice the card
  clock in those three cases.

**HS200 is SDR and is in none of the three.** So there is no UM line that
carries the mechanism for HS200, and this header does not invent one. What the
UM does establish is that the halving exists as a concept in this controller and
that it is enumerated by mode, not implied by MODE_SEL in general.

WHAT SILICON CARRIES
--------------------
HS200 itself was measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-19, for the first time on this
board, from a boot label whose only difference from the delivery one is a DT
with mmc-hs400-1_8v removed:

    dmesg                     mmc0: new HS200 MMC card at address 0001
    debugfs mmc0/ios          clock 200000000, actual clock 200000000,
                              timing spec 9 (mmc HS200), 1.80 V
    CCU SMHC2_CLK  0x02002D20 = 0x81000003   -> 200 MHz module clock
    SMHC_CLKDIV    0x04022004 = 0x00010000   CCLK_ENB=1, CCLK_DIV=0
    SMHC_NTSR      0x0402205C = 0x81710000   MODE_SEL[31]=1, new timing ON
    EMMC_DSBD      0x0402210C = 0x00000000   HS400_MD_EN=0 (correct for HS200)
    raw-region benchmark, 2 GiB raw region behind eMMC p3, performance, cpu7:
                              seqwrite 1 MiB  83.8 MiB/s, verify_bad = 0
                              seqread  1 MiB  88.4 MiB/s

All registers read under load, because the block reads zero when the module
clock is gated. So: module clock 200 MHz, new timing mode on, CCLK_DIV 0, and
the driver believing the card clock is 200 MHz.

88.4 MiB/s on an 8-bit bus is 93 % of the ceiling at a 100 MHz card clock and
46 % of the ceiling at 200 MHz, on a card and controller that reach roughly
70 % of the HS400 ceiling in the harder mode. A 100 MHz card clock fits; a
200 MHz one would require the simpler mode to be half as efficient as the
harder one. That is an inference from a throughput number, not a reading of the
card clock, and it is stated as one.

The second piece of silicon is one SDR mode on this controller family that has
already been measured and did need the doubling: the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-08, SD slot in
SDR104, from `mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth` --

    SMHC0_CLK 0x81000001   M=1, 200 MHz module    45.1 MB/s
    SMHC0_CLK 0x81000000   M=0, 400 MHz module    80.6 MB/s

same card, same slot, same method, and mmc->actual_clock read 200 MHz in both.
45.1 MB/s is 4-bit at 100 MHz; 80.6 is 4-bit at 200. That is the new timing path
halving an SDR mode's clock -- the mechanism this patch assumes for HS200, from
a measurement on a different instance in a different mode, not from the manual.

Taken together: HS200 has been running at a 100 MHz card clock on an 8-bit bus,
a 100 MB/s ceiling, while the driver, the debugfs ios file and every number
taken from them said 200 MHz and 200 MB/s.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
-----------------
One term in the doubling condition, plus the instrument that makes the answer
readable: a dev_info naming the module clock, the divider, the resulting card
clock and CCLK_DIV, printed for the modes where a divider is claimed at all.
mmc->actual_clock cannot serve that purpose -- it is rate/div and therefore
reports what the driver believes, which is how SDR104 hid for six weeks.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH: SMHC_NTSR[31] AND THE 2x CLOCK
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Silicon under load on the 8 GiB board reads SMHC_NTSR 0x81710000 for us against 0x01710000
for the vendor: ours is the UM reset default with MODE_SEL = 1 (new timing),
the vendor deviates to MODE_SEL = 0 (old timing) and writes this eMMC faster.
That is not changed here and must not be changed on its own, because MODE_SEL
and the divider are one setting in two registers: clearing MODE_SEL selects the
1x sample/output timing, and every mode this function doubles the module clock
for is doubled on the assumption of the 2x path. Clearing one without the other
leaves the card clocked at twice or half its intended rate with no error
anywhere.

A board experiment that touches it has to move all of the following together,
in one kernel, and read all of them back at the register under load:

  1. SMHC_NTSR[31] MODE_SEL     1 -> 0
  2. the div/clock doubling in this function, for the mode under test, 2 -> 1
     (so the module clock stays at the card clock)
  3. SMHC_CLKDIV[7:0] CCLK_DIV  -- stays 0 in HS400 (the UM forbids anything
     else while HS400_MD_EN is set) and is the knob that would otherwise have
     to absorb the change in the other modes
  4. SMHC_DRV_DL[17] DAT_DRV_PH_SEL and [16] CMD_DRV_PH_SEL -- Table 11-9
     gives different phase offsets for the 1x and 2x rows, so the drive phase
     that is correct at 2x is not the one that is correct at 1x
  5. the data-strobe sweep (HS400) and the sample-delay sweep, both of which
     are functions of the module clock and must be allowed to re-run; no
     constant from a 2x run may be carried into a 1x run

and it has to be judged on a *write* benchmark, because that is where the
deficit is. Anything less than all five is not an experiment, it is a boot that
happens to survive.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
UM facts cited above (a733-um-v0.92, 11.3.8.2, 11.3.8.3, 11.3.8.23, 11.3.5.6 /
Table 11-9). The SDR104 numbers are from `mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth`'s silicon run on the 8 GiB board,
2026-08-08. The NTSR readback was taken on the board.
No value is taken from any kernel.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies clean at fuzz 0 on a fresh unpack with the series in order: yes.
- Builds: NOT claimed, not built here.
- Silicon: NOT proven, and this patch is written so that one session proves or
  refutes it. The falsifier, on the 8 GiB board, eMMC forced to HS200 (U-Boot:
  "fdt rm /soc/mmc@4022000 mmc-hs400-1_8v"), both arms from one image with
  A7S_SKIP_PATCHES set to this patch's name:

    * read SMHC2_CLK at the CCU under load -- the block is runtime gated and
      reads all-zero when idle -- and record M and the source select;
    * record sequential read and write MiB/s from the same raw-region
      benchmark used for the earlier measurement.

  With the patch, SMHC2_CLK must move from 0x81000003 to a 400 MHz module clock
  and the console must print "module 400000000 Hz / 2 = card 200000000 Hz".
  The row that decides is the throughput: read must leave 88.4 MiB/s and move
  toward 150-180. If it does not move, HS200 was already running at 200 MHz,
  this patch is wrong -- revert it -- and the SMHC_TMOUT enumeration was the
  correct reading after all. Write (83.8 MiB/s) is the second row and the one
  raised in an earlier bug report; it may move less, because the write path's per-block cost is
  a separate question from the bus rate.

ISSUE
-----
"Correct and fast in HS400 and in HS200" cannot be claimed while
nobody knows what the HS200 card clock is.

AMENDED: THE LINE PRINTS ON A CHANGE, NOT ON EVERY CALL
-------------------------------------------------------
The HS200 arm is HW-proven (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19), so the line
stays and its visibility goes down. Runtime PM
autosuspends the host after 50 ms and sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume() re-runs
sunxi_mmc_set_clk() with unchanged ios on every wake-up, so the line reached
the 115200-baud console about 1200 times per 100 min of uptime. Measured on
the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-23, first 4 KiB O_DIRECT read after a 1 s pause,
median: 12.5 ms with runtime PM auto and console loglevel 7, 2.5 ms with
runtime PM held on, 2.6 ms with PM auto and loglevel 5. The ~10 ms is the
synchronous console write; the resume itself costs ~0.1-0.2 ms.

The host now keeps the last timing, card clock and CCLK_DIV it saw. The line
is dev_info when any of the three differs from the last call and dev_dbg
otherwise (dyndbg func sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate brings every call back).
Runtime PM behaviour is not changed.

Amendment verified: applies at fuzz 0 in series on a fresh 6.18.36 unpack.
Not proven on silicon. Falsifier: after boot the clk line appears once per
real change of timing/clock, not per resume; the first read after a pause is
~2.5 ms with console loglevel 7.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -462,6 +462,16 @@
 	unsigned int	samp_dl_clock;
 
 	/*
+	 * What the "clk:" line in sunxi_mmc_clk_set_rate() last saw: the
+	 * timing, the resulting card clock and CCLK_DIV. Runtime resume
+	 * re-runs that function with unchanged ios on every wake-up, so only
+	 * a change is worth dev_info.
+	 */
+	unsigned char	clk_last_timing;
+	long		clk_last_rate;
+	u32		clk_last_cclk_div;
+
+	/*
 	 * Hardware busy wait (cfg->can_wait_busy). busy_wait is set while a
 	 * request has finished on the bus and the card still holds DAT0;
 	 * busy_seq tells busy_work which wait it was started for, because the
@@ -1580,7 +1590,45 @@
 	     * 1x and nobody checked whether SDR104 had the same defect.
 	     */
 	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_UHS_SDR104 ||
-	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_UHS_SDR50) {
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_UHS_SDR50 ||
+	    /*
+	     * HS200 is the same question one more mode over, and the UM does
+	     * not answer it. The only place the A733 UM documents a factor of
+	     * two between the card-clock time base and real time is the
+	     * SMHC_TMOUT note (11.3.8.3): the effective timeout is only half of
+	     * the programmed value in three cases, DDR8 mode, DDR4 with SMHC_NTSR[MODE_SELEC]
+	     * (0x5C[31]) high, and HS400 with
+	     * SMHC_NTSR[HS400_NEW_SAMPLE_EN](0x5C[0]) high. HS200 is SDR and
+	     * appears in none of the three, and MODE_SEL itself is described
+	     * purely as a sample/output *timing* selector (11.3.8.23) with no
+	     * frequency or ratio attached.
+	     *
+	     * What says HS200 belongs here is silicon, on this controller
+	     * family and on an SDR mode: the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-08, SD in SDR104 with
+	     * SMHC0_CLK 0x81000001 (200 MHz module) delivered 45.1 MB/s and
+	     * with 0x81000000 (400 MHz module) 80.6 MB/s on the same card,
+	     * while mmc->actual_clock read 200 MHz in both cases (`mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth`). The
+	     * halving is therefore not a DDR property; it is what the new
+	     * timing path does on the way to the card.
+	     *
+	     * HS200 itself, measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-19 with a
+	     * boot label whose only difference is the removed mmc-hs400-1_8v
+	     * property in DT: SMHC2_CLK
+	     * reads 0x81000003 under load, a 200 MHz module clock, while
+	     * /sys/kernel/debug/mmc0/ios reports a 200 MHz card clock -- the
+	     * same shape of disagreement SDR104 had -- and the raw-region
+	     * benchmark returns 88.4 MiB/s read and 83.8 MiB/s write. On an
+	     * 8-bit bus that is 93 % of the ceiling at a 100 MHz card clock
+	     * and 46 % of the ceiling at 200 MHz, on a card that reaches
+	     * ~70 % of its HS400 ceiling. Consistent with 100 MHz, not with
+	     * 200.
+	     *
+	     * Consistent is not proof, which is why this line is the
+	     * experiment and not a fact: with it the module clock is 400 MHz
+	     * and the card clock 200, and A7S_SKIP_PATCHES gives the other
+	     * arm. The commit message names the same falsifier.
+	     */
+	    ios->timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS200) {
 		div = 2;
 		clock <<= 1;
 	}
@@ -1683,6 +1731,39 @@
 	/* And we just enabled our clock back */
 	mmc->actual_clock = rate;
 
+	/*
+	 * The instrument for the paragraph above, and for every future
+	 * argument about this divider. mmc->actual_clock is rate/div, so it
+	 * tells a reader what the driver believes and nothing about what the
+	 * module clock is doing -- which is exactly how SDR104 ran at half its
+	 * reported clock for six weeks (`mmc-sunxi-sd-sdr104-module-clock-and-fifoth`). Print both sides and the
+	 * divider that separates them, for the modes where a divider is
+	 * claimed at all.
+	 *
+	 * The HS200 arm is settled on silicon (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19), so the
+	 * visibility went down, not the line: dev_info only when the timing,
+	 * the card clock or CCLK_DIV differ from the last call, dev_dbg
+	 * otherwise. Runtime resume (autosuspend 50 ms) calls this with the
+	 * same ios after every I/O pause; at console loglevel 7 each dev_info
+	 * was ~10 ms of synchronous 115200-baud output on the first request
+	 * after the pause (the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-23).
+	 */
+	if (div != 1) {
+		if (ios->timing != host->clk_last_timing ||
+		    rate != host->clk_last_rate ||
+		    clkdiv - 1 != host->clk_last_cclk_div)
+			dev_info(mmc_dev(mmc),
+				 "clk: timing %d, module %ld Hz / %u = card %ld Hz, CCLK_DIV %u\n",
+				 ios->timing, rate * div, div, rate, clkdiv - 1);
+		else
+			dev_dbg(mmc_dev(mmc),
+				"clk: timing %d, module %ld Hz / %u = card %ld Hz, CCLK_DIV %u\n",
+				ios->timing, rate * div, div, rate, clkdiv - 1);
+	}
+	host->clk_last_timing = ios->timing;
+	host->clk_last_rate = rate;
+	host->clk_last_cclk_div = clkdiv - 1;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
